.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1204 PM PDT Sat Sep 26 2026
(This evening through Sunday night)

The stratus deck has mixed out to the immediate coast of Marin
County and the Ocean Beach side of San Francisco and lurking near or
just off the coast of San Mateo County. High clouds continue to flow
over the North Bay and will continue to do so through the day, as
the stratus deck returns to many of the coastal valleys this evening
into the overnight period.

A developing trough with an axis around 600 miles west of Cape
Mendocino is approaching the West Coast, digging deeper into the
upper level flow as it does so and eventually dropping deep enough
to cross into the California coast, where it should further develop
into a cut-off low somewhere around the North Coast and the northern
half of the Central Valley through the latter portion of Sunday. The
influence of the cut-off low will bring cooler and more moist
conditions to the region. High temperatures will range around 5 to
15 degrees below seasonal averages for today and Sunday, with highs
in the inland valleys reaching the middle 70s to lower 80s today and
the 70s on Sunday. High temperatures in the warmest portions of
southern Monterey County will range from the middle 80s to lower 90s
today, and the upper 70s to middle 80s on Sunday. Meanwhile, the
Bayshore regions should expect highs in the upper 60s to upper 70s
on Saturday and the middle 60s to middle 70s on Sunday, while the
Pacific coast remains relatively cool with highs in the upper 50s to
middle 60s on both days.

As the cut-off low moves across the state through the weekend,
cooler temperatures and some incoming moisture should result in a
wetter mid-layer, around the 300-500 mb layer. Exceedingly dry lower
layers should cause the evaporation of most precipitation that may
result, but can`t rule out a few sprinkles overnight for the coastal
regions and the highest peaks.

