.FIRE WEATHER...
Issued at 420 AM PDT Sat Sep 26 2026

Our diffuse cold front continues to slowly move southward through
Southern California. In its wake, some areas have benefited from
the cooler and slightly more "moist" conditions this morning. The
exception, however, is across the higher terrain of the Santa
Cruz Mountains as well as the Santa Lucia and Diablo Ranges where
RH recovery remains poor (20-40%). Afternoon humidity will remain
under 40%, largely for areas away from the coast. A push of
onshore flow is expected later this evening into Sunday morning
resulting in the marine layer deepening to around 2,000 feet ahead
of an upper trough. This moisture push should diminish the areal
coverage of poor RH recovery Sunday and Monday morning. A few
sprinkles cannot be ruled out along the highest peaks associated
with this upper low Sunday AM. Dry and modest offshore breezes
will be a possibility Tuesday and into Wednesday, with the
potential for wind gusts above 35 mph being greatest across the
higher terrain of the North Bay in the wake of this upper low.
Farther south across the Central Coast, the primary impact from
this low will be a return to widespread poor RH recovery with some
locales across the Santa Lucia Range struggling to see max RH
above 30% Wednesday morning.

